From: OpenAI Codex <codex@openai.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: drain RX NAPI before reclaiming queue buffers

Keep the upstream order between netif_napi_del() and RX queue cleanup.
The shared RX queue initializer enables threaded NAPI, while older drivers
such as mt7603 and mt7615 do not disable their RX NAPI in removal. Deleting
NAPI stops that thread and waits for a network grace period; reclaiming
buffers and destroying the page pool first lets a running poll race them.

Retain MT7996's tracked disable and the other drivers' existing ownership
of napi_disable(), so already-disabled instances are not disabled twice.

Signed-off-by: OpenAI Codex <codex@openai.com>
---
diff --git a/dma.c b/dma.c
--- a/dma.c
+++ b/dma.c
@@ -1306,20 +1306,21 @@
 		struct mt76_queue *q = &dev->q_rx[i];
 		bool napi_registered = test_bit(i, &dev->napi_registered);
 
-		/* Untracked drivers disable RX NAPI in their own remove path. */
+		/* Keep each driver's existing RX-disable ownership. Deleting
+		 * NAPI must still drain its thread and poll tails before buffers.
+		 */
 		if (napi_registered && dev->napi_state_tracked)
 			mt76_napi_disable(dev, i, &dev->napi[i]);
+		if (napi_registered) {
+			netif_napi_del(&dev->napi[i]);
+			clear_bit(i, &dev->napi_registered);
+		}
 		if (q->ndesc)
 			mt76_dma_rx_cleanup(dev, q);
 
 		if (q->page_pool) {
 			page_pool_destroy(q->page_pool);
 			q->page_pool = NULL;
-		}
-
-		if (napi_registered) {
-			netif_napi_del(&dev->napi[i]);
-			clear_bit(i, &dev->napi_registered);
 		}
 	}
 
